|> Section
    title=The beta distribution

    Define the $\textbf{Euler beta function}$ (which is
    closely related to the Euler gamma function) by

    $$
    B(a,b) = \int_0^1 u^{a-1}(1-u)^{b-1}\,du \qquad (a,b>0).
    $$

    $\textbf{Properties of the beta function:}$

    |> ol
        class=list
        data-list-style=decimal
        |> li

            $B(a,b) = \frac{\Gamma(a) \Gamma(b)}{\Gamma(a+b)}$.

        |> li
            For integer $m,n\ge 1$,
            $B(m,n) = \frac{(m-1)! (n-1)!}{(m+n-1)!}$.

    We say that $X$ has the beta distribution with
    parameters $a,b>0$, and denote
    $X\sim \operatorname{Beta}(a,b)$, if $X$ has density
    function

    $$
    f_X(x) = \frac{1}{B(a,b)} x^{a-1} (1-x)^{b-1} \qquad (0<x<1).
    $$

    $\textbf{Properties of the beta distribution:}$

    |> ol
        class=list
        data-list-style=decimal
        |> li
            $U[0,1] \stackrel{d}{=} \operatorname{Beta}(1,1)$.

        |> li
            If $X \sim \operatorname{Gamma}(\alpha,\lambda)$ and
            $Y\sim \operatorname{Gamma}(\beta,\lambda)$ are
            independent, then $U = \frac{X}{X+Y}$ has
            distribution $\operatorname{Beta}(\alpha,\beta)$, and
            is independent of $X+Y$.

        |> li
            If $X_1,X_2,\ldots$ is a sequence of i.i.d. r.v.s
            with distribution $\operatorname{Exp}(\lambda)$, and
            $S_m = \sum_{k=1}^m X_k$ are the cumulative sums of
            the sequence, then for all $n\ge k\ge1$,
            $S_k/S_n \sim \operatorname{Beta}(k,n-k)$, and
            $S_k/S_n$ is independent of $S_n$.

        |> li
            If $X\sim \operatorname{Beta}(a,b)$ then
            $\mathbf{E} X = \frac{a}{a+b}$,
            $\mathbf{V}(X) = \frac{ab}{(a+b)^2 (a+b+1)}$.

        |> li
            If $X_1,\ldots,X_n$ are i.i.d. $U[0,1]$ random
            variables, and $X^{(1)} < X^{(2)} < \ldots < X^{(n)}$
            are their order statistics, i.e., $X^{(k)}$ is
            defined as the $k$th smallest among the numbers
            $X_1,\ldots,X_n$, then
            $X^{(k)} \sim \operatorname{Beta}(k,n+1-k)$.